        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "d5125e42-f377-5eeb-9257-e41e2597054d",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Plants/Genera: Crepis, Isatis, Hieracium\n- People: E. B. Babcock\n- Families: Asteraceae, Brassicaceae\n- Concepts: polyploidy, apomixis\n\nFacts:\n- Crepis is a genus of annual and perennial flowering plants in the family Asteraceae.\n- Crepis is commonly called hawksbeard or hawk's-beard in some parts of the world.\n- Crepis superficially resembles the dandelion, but it usually has branching scapes with multiple heads, although solitary heads can occur.\n- Hieracium is a related genus with a similar common name to Crepis.\n- Isatis is a genus of about 30 species of flowering plants in the family Brassicaceae.\n- Isatis is native from the Mediterranean region east to central Asia.\n- E. B. Babcock is particularly known for field investigations and extensive experiments on the entire polyploid apomictic genus Crepis.\n- E. B. Babcock recognized 196 species in Crepis.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "5b10ed8b-c8e0-539c-93ee-9fb097429f50",
        "text": "Page: Crepis. Sentence: Crepis, commonly known in some parts of the world as hawksbeard or hawk's-beard (but not to be confused with the related genus \"Hieracium\" with a similar common name), is a genus of annual and perennial flowering plants of the family Asteraceae superficially resembling the dandelion, the most conspicuous difference being that \"Crepis\" usually has branching scapes with multiple heads (though solitary heads can occur).\nPage: Isatis. Sentence: Isatis is a genus of about 30 species of flowering plants in the family Brassicaceae, native to the Mediterranean region east to central Asia.\nPage: E. B. Babcock. Sentence: He is particularly known for seeking to understand by field investigations and extensive experiments, the entire polyploid apomictic genus \"Crepis\", in which he recognize 196 species.",
